<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pergamon Altar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 180–160 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Great Altar of Pergamon, Great Altar of Zeus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> Altar is a monumental altar built on the acropolis of Pergamon in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a> in the first half of the 2nd century BCE. Its frieze, about 113 m long, shows the battle of the gods and giants, a symbol of Attalid victories over the Galatians. Excavated by German archaeologists from 1878, it was reconstructed in the Pergamon Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamenes">Pergamene</a> kingdom, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philetaerus">Philetaerus</a> at the start of the 3rd century BCE, was first part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>. Attalus I won full independence and proclaimed himself king after defeating the Celtic Galatians in 228 BCE. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a> secured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea">Treaty of Apamea</a> in 188 BCE as an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalid-dynasty">Attalids</a> wished to show their importance through imposing buildings. Until the later 20th century some scholars thought the altar was endowed in 184 BCE after a victory over the Tolistoagian tribe, but study of its construction and friezes showed that it was not conceived as a monument to a particular victory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The structure measured 35.74 metres wide and 33.4 metres deep, and its front stairway was almost 20 metres wide. A high-relief frieze on the base shows the battle of the Olympian gods against the Giants, children of Gaia, the Gigantomachy. A smaller frieze in the inner court told the life of Telephus, founder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> and son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> and Auge. It is probably the altar of a temple, perhaps that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> on the terrace above, and not a temple itself, but no theory is generally accepted. Wolfgang Radt concluded that no research is undisputed concerning its builder, date, occasion or purpose.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The altar is mentioned in antiquity only by the Roman Lucius Ampelius, who called it a great marble altar 40 feet high, and by Pausanias. In the 7th century the acropolis was fortified against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and the altar partly destroyed for building material. In 1625 William Petty took two relief panels to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> for the Earl of Arundel; forgotten, they were rediscovered in the 1960s, so they are missing from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> reconstruction. The German engineer Carl Humann began official excavations in 1878 and they lasted until 1886, and the relief panels were transferred to Berlin. Many scholars have also identified the altar with the Seat of Satan in the Book of Revelation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pergamon Altar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Great Altar of Zeus</b> celebrated victory over the Galatians.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-pergamon">Kingdom of Pergamon</a></li></ul></section>
